Drs: "doctorandus", latin for "doctor to be".

Study at university for four years minimum and get your degree, and
you're either a mr. (master, in law), an ir. (engineer, five years
minimum), or a drs. (doctorandus, all other studies). Call it a
bachelor, if you wish (it's a little bit more).

After becoming a drs., almost everyone finishes their studies. Some
continue to write a thesis, which is reviewed by a professor. When it is
approved (this may take two years, and some older people take ten years
or more to finish it in the evening hours), you get to be a dr.
(doctor). Comparable to a master, in the anglosaxon model.

Since five years or so, you may, if you wish, call yourself bachelor or
master instead of doctorandus or doctor, since those titles are better
understood abroad.

